<info xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
      xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://apps.nextcloud.com/schema/apps/info.xsd">
  <id>penpot_sync</id>
  <name>Penpot Sync</name>
  <summary>Your Penpot design files as native, read-only, backed-up Nextcloud files</summary>
  <description><![CDATA[
Penpot Sync mirrors your Penpot design files into Nextcloud as native files. A
scheduled pull walks your mapped Penpot teams/projects and every design file
appears in Nextcloud as a `.penpot` file — a real file icon, a deep link back to
the live design in Penpot, and a local backup of the exported archive.

Unlike this app's sibling integrations for n8n and Grafana, Penpot Sync is
**read-only**: there is no "Edit as text" action and no Nextcloud → Penpot
writeback. A `.penpot` export is an opaque archive of nested design data —
editing it outside Penpot wouldn't produce anything sane to re-import. Design
happens in Penpot; Nextcloud holds the backup and the click-through.

⚠️ **Very early development.** The admin surface is complete — instance URL,
service-account token, team mappings, scheduled-pull settings, and an optional
per-user token for attribution — and every control has an `occ` twin. But there
is still **no sync engine and no file actions: nothing is mirrored yet.** You
can configure the app fully and verify it reaches your Penpot
(`occ penpot_sync:test-connection` reports which teams the account can see), and
that is all it does today. See the project's `saga/` design narrative for what's
confirmed, what's locked, and what's still an open question.

The `enable-access-tokens` Penpot instance flag is required to mint the token;
it is off by default upstream.

This is a community integration and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or
sponsored by Penpot (Kaleidos Ventures SL). "Penpot" and the Penpot logo are
trademarks of their respective owner, used here only to identify the service
this app integrates with.
  ]]></description>
  <version>0.1.0</version>
  <licence>agpl</licence>
  <author>kubed-io</author>
  <namespace>PenpotSync</namespace>
  <category>integration</category>
  <category>files</category>
  <!--
    DECLARED SO THE APP IS LOADED ON WEBDAV REQUESTS (saga §C6.13).

    `register()` runs for every enabled app during bootstrap, but `boot()` only
    runs when an app is actually LOADED — and remote.php, the WebDAV entry point,
    loads a restricted set:

        $appManager->loadApps(['authentication']);
        $appManager->loadApps(['extended_authentication']);
        $appManager->loadApps(['filesystem', 'logging']);

    With no <types>, this app was never in that set, so boot() never ran on a DAV
    request. Everything wired in register() — the copy, move, rename, create and
    soft-delete listeners — worked fine, which is what made the gap so hard to
    see: only the ONE thing wired in boot(), the legacy trash-purge hook, was
    silently dead. Emptying the trash left the design in Penpot forever.

    `filesystem` is the type for apps that must be present when the filesystem is
    in play, which is exactly what a file-event-driven mirror is.
  -->
  <types>
    <filesystem/>
  </types>
  <website>https://github.com/kubed-io/nextcloud-penpot</website>
  <bugs>https://github.com/kubed-io/nextcloud-penpot/issues</bugs>
  <repository type="git">https://github.com/kubed-io/nextcloud-penpot.git</repository>
  <dependencies>
    <!-- 32, not 30, since the project-folder opt-in landed: `OCP\SystemTag\TagAssignedEvent`
         is @since 32.0.0, and on 30/31 the listener would simply never fire — the tag
         would sit on the folder doing nothing, with no error anywhere. A silently
         absent feature is the failure mode this project keeps having to dig out, so
         the floor is raised to where the feature actually works. (Live is 33.) -->
    <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
  </dependencies>
  <settings>
    <!-- Section order (by priority), deliberately identical to nextcloud-n8n and
         nextcloud-grafana so an admin who has configured one already knows where
         to look here:

           Instance (5)      base URL + service-account token, one card
           Sync Settings (20) the scheduled pull
           Team mappings (30) the repeating mapping cards
           Sync Actions (45)  every button in the section, rendered last

         Instance and Sync Settings are DECLARATIVE forms, registered in
         AppInfo\Application::register(). Team mappings and Sync Actions are
         classic panels (declarative settings can host neither an
         array-of-objects nor a button), so they are listed here.

         AdminTest is NOT listed: it exists only as the #[AuthorizedAdminSetting]
         target for the test endpoint, and renders nothing. Same arrangement as
         nextcloud-grafana. -->
    <admin>OCA\PenpotSync\Settings\MappingSettings</admin>
    <admin>OCA\PenpotSync\Settings\SyncSettings</admin>
    <admin-section>OCA\PenpotSync\Settings\AdminSection</admin-section>
    <personal-section>OCA\PenpotSync\Settings\PersonalSection</personal-section>
  </settings>
  <commands>
    <!-- CLI-first is the house style: every control on the admin surface has a
         headless twin, which is what the integration suite drives and what makes
         declarative cluster config possible. -->
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\SetUrl</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\SetToken</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\SetPersonalToken</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\ShowConfig</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\Probe</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\TestConnection</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\ListTeams</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\AddMapping</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\ListMappings</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\RemoveMapping</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\SetGroups</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\Sync</command>
    <command>OCA\PenpotSync\Command\Status</command>
  </commands>
  <repair-steps>
    <!--
      Installs the application/vnd.penpot mimetype + icon mapping on every
      install/upgrade. Idempotent; see lib/Migration/RegisterMimetype.php.
    -->
    <install>
      <step>OCA\PenpotSync\Migration\RegisterMimetype</step>
    </install>
    <post-migration>
      <step>OCA\PenpotSync\Migration\RegisterMimetype</step>
    </post-migration>
    <uninstall>
      <!-- Mirror image of RegisterMimetype: reverts the core-tree mimetype/icon
           registration so removing the app leaves core as it found it. Never
           touches the user's .penpot files, their metadata, or Penpot.
           See lib/Migration/UnregisterMimetype.php + uninstall.feature. -->
      <step>OCA\PenpotSync\Migration\UnregisterMimetype</step>
    </uninstall>
  </repair-steps>
</info>
